## Relations the ontology can hold

The ontology was extended with a field for every pair of classes the training split relates,
by these rules:

- the field belongs to the class of the whole and holds a list of parts, named after the
  part's class in the plural;
- a whole holding parts of a class and of its subclass holds both through one field, typed by
  the superclass;
- a class holding itself gets no field;
- a relation that is not one of part and whole gets no field: a thing hanging on or resting
  in the whole (clothing, a towel and a curtain on a door, an accessory hung on a door, a key
  card in its slot, a shower head in its holder, an intercom handset in its cradle, a
  thermometer on a heater), and a part annotated under the wrong whole (a flush plate under
  a toilet lid, a faucet head under a drain pipe); their relations count as ones no field
  holds;
- where a part is attached to its whole the other way round from how it belongs to it, the
  field is declared on the semantic whole and its parts carry that whole: mounting a part
  hangs the whole from it, as annotated.

